  "cause": "France answered last weekend's destruction with a number, and the number worked — for one night. **After a day of largely peaceful demonstrations, police fired tear gas and water cannon to disperse protesters in Paris, barricades were set alight in Marseille and people ripped boards from a shop in Bordeaux — but officials managed to avoid rioting and looting on the scale of the previous weekend, which produced some of the worst disorder in France's modern history. The interior ministry estimated 410,000 protesters nationwide on Saturday; the unions put the figure at about 800,000. Some 70,000 police officers were deployed, and restrictions were imposed in several cities, including President Emmanuel Macron's decree that only demonstrations organised by a recognised trade union would be permitted** (BBC). **Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez said \"several thousand radical activists gathered at the rear of the march, including around 500 individuals identified as rioters\". The ministry counted 142 arrests in the capital on Saturday — less than half the figure for the same day a week earlier — while the national tally approached 1,000. Two people were seriously injured: a 20-year-old protester in Toulouse who, Franceinfo reported, had several fingers torn off by a nearby explosion, and another in Clermont-Ferrand. In Bouches-du-Rhône, seven businesses were \"looted or damaged\". Clashes in Paris came at the end of a march from Porte d'Italie to Porte de Vincennes, where the police operation included an elite unit usually reserved for organised crime and counter-terrorism; in Lyon, tear gas was used on a crowd that had gathered in an area the prefecture had declared off-limits** (BBC/AFP). **The movement has now spread past the country that started it.** **Protests also took place in other European countries over the same weekend, with young demonstrators returning to the streets in Italy — fed up, as they put it, with the political establishment and the rising cost of living — after violence overshadowed their protests the previous weekend** (BBC; NYT). **And the demands have not moved from where the movement began: \"We were really frustrated… They keep us quiet. They take our money, they tax us a lot and we can't say anything. Today we are in the streets because it's been too long, it's been too much,\" said Zelie, 26, in Paris; two masked teenage demonstrators said their generation felt abandoned, and one of them, asked about last Saturday's looting, answered: \"I think looting is really not good… But it's because of poverty and the violence of the police.\" The protests, he said, would end \"when we have a response to our demands\"** (BBC).",
