  "cause": "Two ledgers of this war have been running for weeks — the dead and the displaced. A third opened this week, and it is written in oil. **Two people were killed in a Houthi attack on King Fahd International Airport in Dammam, eastern Saudi Arabia, the Saudi General Authority of Civil Aviation said, identifying them as a Saudi citizen and an Indian resident. It did not say what type of attack occurred, but the Houthis said on Thursday that the airport was one of the sites they had targeted with drones and missiles over the previous 48 hours. Houthi military spokesman Yahya Sarea said they had also targeted the Saudi defence ministry's headquarters in Riyadh, an Aramco facility in the Red Sea city of Yanbu, and Abha International Airport in the south; the Saudi-led coalition said four drones were intercepted over Eastern Province on Wednesday night and four more over Riyadh and Medina on Thursday** (BBC). **Two further people were killed on Wednesday morning by debris from a drone intercepted as it attempted to target a processing facility at Aramco's Shaybah oil field, where a contractor's vehicle was set ablaze — residents of the Philippines and Nepal, the Saudi energy ministry said. At least 21 people have now been killed in Houthi attacks on Saudi airports since the start of October; the 10 October missile strike on Riyadh's international airport killed 13 and injured 308, the deadliest attack in the kingdom in more than a decade** (BBC). **And on the water, a tanker that was hit at the start of October has now arrived on someone's beach.** **Satellite images reviewed by BBC Verify and the intelligence firm MAIAR show a slick from a ship matching the location and dimensions of the Panama-flagged crude tanker Gem No.2 — struck by an unknown projectile on 9 October north of Al Jazeera Al Hamra, all 22 Indian crew rescued — spreading more than 35km by Wednesday, and UAE authorities said on Thursday that it was affecting beaches near the northern city of Umm Al Quwain. The UAE's Ministry of Climate Change and Environment said it \"immediately began containment\" in order to \"limit environmental impacts\". The spill extends towards Siniyah and Khor Al Beidah islands, a globally recognised Key Biodiversity Area containing corals, seagrasses and saltmarshes and species including green turtles and the critically endangered Halavi guitarfish. \"It is inevitable that there will be an impact,\" Prof Steve Widdicombe of Sharjah Marine Science Research Centre told BBC Verify, while Eoghan Darbyshire of the Conflict and Environment Observatory called the spill's proximity to that area \"the most concerning environmental angle\"** (BBC Verify). **Gem No.2 was one of a record number of ships attacked in and around the Strait of Hormuz at the start of October — attacks security experts describe as part of an Iranian effort to assert control of the strait in response to the US military escorting more vessels through it** (BBC Verify).",
