  "cause": "The tariff story has acquired a second front, and this one is about testimony rather than plywood. **The US Embassy in Ottawa has accused Canadian officials of election meddling ahead of the November midterms, saying Canadian leaders have brushed off concerns over \"potential interference\" and that ministers and premiers \"openly describe plans to influence American politics in official documents and public appearances\". The fact sheet released on Wednesday cites meetings with US Democrats and calls for retaliatory tariffs. It quotes Industry Minister Mélanie Joly, asked what she expected regarding tariffs if Democrats were to win: \"That's exactly why I'm meeting so many governors and potential presidential candidates.\" It says Ontario Premier Doug Ford pushed the federal government to consider additional retaliatory tariffs on imports from \"politically significant states to the current administration's base of support\"** (BBC). **Prime Minister Mark Carney dismissed the claims and noted that it was the United States that first initiated trade actions against Canada: \"We've responded to those to protect Canadian jobs, to protect Canadian companies and develop our economy.\" He spoke while announcing a nearly C$9bn ($6.2bn) aid package in response to US tariffs, accompanied by Joly and Ford; Ford said he has \"no interest at all\" in interfering in US elections — \"I'll tell you what the American people want. They want certainty, they want stability… The people of the United States will make that determination in the midterms.\" Joly denied interference last week, telling CBC that Canadians \"never accept that also being done to us in Canada\" and that her US visits were \"to attract investments\"** (BBC/CBC). **This sits on top of a tariff regime that is itself being administered by announcement.** **From 30 October, the Trump administration is easing some of its 50% tariffs on Canadian goods — plywood sheets, some paper products, certain machinery and refrigeration equipment — after Republican Senator Susan Collins of Maine, in a tight race, said she had lobbied for the change, while introducing new tariffs on carpets and \"waste products\" in the same announcement. The 50% levy had been imposed on $20bn of Canadian goods after trade talks collapsed in August, and Canada answered with countermeasures Carney called \"necessary to protect our workers\"** (BBC). **The context on both sides is electoral: the midterms will decide which party controls Congress; US Ambassador to Canada Pete Hoekstra warned earlier this month that officials were \"closely watching\" Canadian politicians for signs of interference; and Trump administration officials have themselves been accused of inappropriate remarks about Alberta's separation referendum** (BBC).",
