  "cause": "The cluster has a genealogy now, and the genealogy leaves the country. **Kenya has recorded its second Ebola death: a woman aged 65, a close family member of the first victim — a man who travelled from the Democratic Republic of Congo — died after being placed in quarantine, the health ministry said. She developed symptoms on 13 October. Seven other patients were receiving treatment at two isolation facilities: three other relatives of the index case, three health workers and a hospital cleaner. The ministry said all cases were within \"one cluster, with no evidence of community spread\"** (BBC). **More than 4,000 people have died in Africa since Ebola was confirmed in DR Congo in May, making this the second deadliest outbreak of the disease on record. The Bundibugyo species involved is relatively rare, with no approved vaccine or treatment, though trials are under way. With more than 9,500 cases in DR Congo, Kenya faces a steady risk of new cases arriving across borders, according to the International Rescue Committee** (BBC/IRC). **And Nigeria's single case has been genetically linked to Kenya's — through a shared airport.** **Nigeria's health minister, Prof Ali Pate, said its sole confirmed case appeared to be genetically related to the strain identified earlier in Kenya, and that there was no evidence so far that the Kenyan patient had travelled to Nigeria — but that further testing was under way. Officials are investigating whether the two crossed paths, since both are believed to have travelled through Uganda's Entebbe airport earlier this month. Uganda's health ministry announced on Friday that people who had travelled to DR Congo would no longer be able to transit via Uganda. Kenya has introduced enhanced screening for travellers from DR Congo and nine other high-risk countries — Uganda, Tanzania, Rwanda, Burundi, Angola, Zambia, Ethiopia, South Sudan and the Central African Republic — with a digital surveillance form completed before boarding and a QR code presented to port health officials on arrival. The US has warned its citizens against travelling to Kenya, while Uganda, Ethiopia and Somalia are on alert** (BBC).",
