  "cause": "For a week, Ukraine's drones could not fly at the speed its operators needed, and the reason turned out to be a clause. **Elon Musk said on Saturday that SpaceX restricted Ukraine's Starlink connection because the Ukrainian military had violated the service's terms: \"Using Starlink, a civilian commercial service, for offensive drone strikes to kill people has never been allowed, nor should it be… The loophole was closed last week.\" He denied responsibility for a simultaneous outage of Starshield, the Pentagon-funded, military-grade counterpart, saying it was controlled by the US government and \"what they decide to do is up to them\". A later post drew the line he is defending: Starlink \"can be used for infrastructure that is military, but it cannot be used for unequivocally offensive strikes that make it fair game to be struck back\"** (BBC). **Musk had earlier denied deliberately throttling Ukraine, which said the restriction hampered defensive operations for more than a week. His latest comments, and the apparent restoration of service, came after President Donald Trump said he had told Musk to \"put it back\", having repeatedly denied instructing him to limit the service** (BBC). **The Ukrainians' answer was technical, and it is the more damaging part of the exchange.** **Ukrainian presidential adviser Serhii Beskrestnov called Musk's post \"very strange\", noting that Starlink terminals operating in Ukraine have a speed restriction of 70–90km/h and do not work on Russian territory: \"Therefore, we physically cannot use commercial Starlink for deep strikes and medium-range strikes. It is very strange that Elon Musk is not aware of Starlink restrictions in Ukraine.\" Defence Minister Yevhenii Khmara said the military was recording a \"stable connection\", with drones able to reach speeds above 400km/h — up from a cutoff Kyiv said had been at 161km/h. \"The speeds are now where they need to be,\" President Volodymyr Zelensky wrote on Telegram. The restrictions had coincided with a tense diplomatic standoff between Zelensky and Trump over Ukrainian strikes on Russian oil infrastructure, which Kyiv says are crucial to stemming Moscow's main revenue source and which Trump said had pushed global oil prices higher** (BBC).",
